[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Current state is healthy. The inter-domain routing “handshake” (BGP peering) between the border routers is up and exchanging routes, and end-to-end connectivity from your pc1 to the partner’s pc2 works.

Evidence:
- From pc1 to pc2 (195.11.14.2 -> 200.1.1.2) ping succeeds with 0% loss and ~1.3 ms RTT; reverse also succeeds.
- router1 “show ip route” contains B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 on eth0; router2 contains B>* 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1. This proves each side learned the other’s LAN via BGP.
- Both routers’ “show ip bgp summary” show the single neighbor established with 1 prefix received and 2 sent; the Up/Down timer is ~00:01:29, indicating the session came up about 90 seconds before our check.

Mechanism and localization: If the BGP session on the inter-router link (router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0, neighbors 193.10.11.1/193.10.11.2) were down, neither router would have the remote LAN route, and pings would fail. Since BGP is established and routes are installed, traffic forwards correctly.

Root cause: No active fault found; the adjacency appears to have (re)established recently, possibly after a transient or service restart.

Corrective fix: No action required now. Recommend monitoring FRR logs for prior flaps and ensuring FRR services are enabled to start at boot. If issues recur, collect bgpd logs and neighbor timers for analysis.
